|weaponinfo = 
The Heckler & Koch HK416 is a collaboration project between the US Army Delta Force R&D department and the German firearms company Heckler & Koch, at the request of US Army combat veteran Larry Vickers. The goal was to make a M4 variant that has a shorter profile and lacks the AR-15's direct impingement system. The rifle was originally named the HKM4, but was later changed to HK416 after a law suit was filed by Colt Defense over the trademark for the M4 name. The project took inspiration from the HK50, XM8, and L85A2 endeavours of H&K. 

Chambered in 5.56x45mm NATO, the HK416 is a short-stroke gas piston operated, rotating bolt, selective fire automatic carbine. Feeding from NATO STANAG magazines, the rifle has a rate of fire of 700-900 rounds per minute. The rifle's foldable iron sights provide accurate fire out to 300 meters. It features a proprietary short-stroke piston mechanism developed by H&K for the G36 as opposed to the direct gas impingement system used by most AR-15 derivatives. This piston system enhances the rifle's reliability once it is dipped in water or sand. It has an adjustable telescopic buttstock.

The rifle features Picatinny rail placements along the top of the upper receiver, as well as on the top, bottom, and sides of the RIS handguard. The rails are compatible with various accessories such as optical sights, lasers, and flashlights. The handguard does not come in contact with the barrel, which is "free-floating" to improve the rifle's accuracy by reducing the impact of the handguard's movements on the barrel. The bottom rails can mount a fore-grip or a 40mm grenade launcher (typically the AG36 or M320 launchers).

Military/Police units all over the world are adopting HK416 variants due to their reliability, performance, and modularity. Starting with the Norwegian army (HK416N), the United States special operation groups such as Delta Force and DEVGRU SEALs have followed suit and begun the process of phasing out their M4A1 carbines for HK416s. The French army is also planning on replacing their FAMAS F1 with the HK416F.<ref name = "416 wiki">[[wikipedia:Heckler & Koch HK416|Wikipedia entry on HK416]]</ref>.

As of 2021, the HK416 A8 (G95A1) was chosen to replace the G36 as the main service rifle of Germany's Bundeswehr after the A5 failed to surpass the G36 in MOA and weight trials. Prior to this, the G38, a strictly semi-automatic variant of the HK416 A5, replaced the MP5 as standard emergency firearm for their police units as the only usage of the HK416 A5 in German service,<ref name = "416 firearmblog">[https://www.thefirearmblog.com/blog/2014/09/01/hk416a5-now-g38/ The Firearm Blog article on HK416/G38]</ref><ref>[https://www.thefirearmblog.com/blog/2019/05/14/hessian-police-select-hk416/ The Firearm Blog article on German police adoption of the G38]</ref> while the HK416 A7 was adopted by the German Special Commando Forces and the German Naval Special Commando Forces under the designation G95 in 2017.<ref>[https://www.thefirearmblog.com/blog/2017/10/12/hk416-a7-called-g95-german-special-forces/ The Firearm Blog article on HK416/G95]</ref>

The US Marine Corps' M27 Infantry Automatic Rifle is a beefed up version of the HK416, designed to partially replace the M249 Squad Automatic Weapon light machine gun in a suppressive fire role. The M27 IAR emphasizes a more marksman approach over the belt-fed M249 SAW, and is more maneuverable in CQB scenarios. It is fitted with a bipod for stability.<ref name = "M27 IAR">[[wikipedia:M27 Infantry Automatic Rifle|Wikipedia entry on M27 IAR]]</ref>

|design =
HK416 uses a HK416D10RS upper with its distinctive handguard mated to a HK416 A5 lower. The HK416 A5 is a slightly updated version of the HK416D, mostly distinguishable visually by the M4 style magwell. Her accessories are Magpull MOE stock and vertical foregrip, a forward mounted Eotech ESP3, a PEQ-15, an AAC M4-2000 Suppressor, and a Surefire scout light. In her Starry Cocoon costume, she trades in the vertical foregrip for an angled foregrip and has a Trijicon ACOG with what appears to be a micro red dot mounted behind, possibly because NIXOO confused it for a magnifier. In HK416's Mod3 artwork, she swaps out the handguard for a modified Geiselle SMR with both a top and bottom rail. In addition to her EXPS3 which is now mounted farther back, she also has a G33 magnifier.

Artist NIXOO commented on the design of HK416 in the Official ''Girls' Frontline'' Art Book<ref name = "artbook">{{cite|ab1|202}}</ref>:

HK416 is an improved model of the AR-15 developed by Heckler & Koch for the US military market. As a result, she is somewhat related to the members of AR team. (HK416是hk公司针对美军市场的改良型AR，和AR小队有一定的渊源，）

I incorporated various design elements from UmiNeko's AR team characters and added various German touches.（所以设计的时候在整体风格上参考了海猫的AR小队设计，并且添加了很多德国元素，）

For example: the German special forces KSK's beret and beret badge, 'Kommando Spezialkräfte' being written on her chest, and the hair pin design being based on the German Iron Cross, while the high collar design was inspired by the Bundeswehr's combat uniform.（比如特种部队KSK的贝雷帽和帽徽，胸前写着Kommando Spezialkraefte<sup>[sic]</sup>，KSK名称的英文<sup>[sic]</sup>拼写，发卡也是由铁十字变形而来。衣领的样式则来自德国国防军作战服特色的高龄。）

By the way, the teardrop on her face symbolizes vengeance~（顺便一提：脸上眼泪团代表复仇~）

The 'blood tear' tattoo under HK416's left eye is likely in reference to HK's defeat in a lawsuit against Colt about copyright infringement over the name "HKM4".

